        "editor": "slate",
        "id": "2770143401",
        "textDirection": "ltr",
        "type": "content"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Octahedral complexes have a coordination number of 6. The six electron donor species are arranged at the corners of an octahedron around the central metal ion, forming bond angles of 90° about the central atom with adjacent ligands. Representations of [Co(H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O)"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "6"
              },
              {
                "text": "]Cl"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": " and [Cr(en)"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": "](NO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": ")"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": ", along with the potassium cations in K"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "[PtCl"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "6"
              },
              {
                "text": "], are shown below. Note that the chloride anions in [Co(H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O)"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "6"
              },
              {
                "text": "]Cl"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": ", the nitrate anions in [Cr(en)"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": "](NO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": ")"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": ", and the potassium cations in K"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "[PtCl"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "6"
              },
              {
                "text": "], are "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "counterions"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "An ion of opposite charge that accompanies a charged species in a compound to maintain electrical neutrality; it balances the charge of complex ions within a coordination compound."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "695913059",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3438831161",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ". These are written outside the brackets and are not covalently bonded to the metal ion."
              }
            ],
            "id": "97107283",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "Many transition metal complexes adopt octahedral geometries, with six donor atoms. Only ligands within the coordination sphere affect the geometry around the metal center."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3793457569",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
